<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dammam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dammam is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the capital of its Eastern Province. In 1938 the well Dammam No. 7, drilled by the American-owned company later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco">Aramco</a>, struck the first commercial oil in the kingdom. Together with Dhahran and Khobar, Dammam has grown into a large metropolitan area and port at the centre of the Saudi oil industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dammam.jpg" alt="Dammam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: علي عدنان الرمضان · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%88%D8%B3%D8%B7_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%85.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dammam is the seat and largest city of the Eastern Province of Saudi Arabia, an industrial port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast. It forms the core of Greater Dammam, which includes the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> of Khobar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a>. The climate is hot desert, with extremely hot summers, sparse rainfall mostly in winter, and dust storms called shamals that generally come from the north in summer. Water comes from desalination plants, and large stretches of the shallow Gulf have been reclaimed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area that became Dammam was settled by the Dawasir tribe around 1923 with the permission of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, and it was originally a fishing hamlet. In 1866 the United Kingdom attacked the fort at Dammam, then held by the Second Saudi state, and was repelled. After the unification of Saudi Arabia Dammam was made capital of the newly formed Eastern Province.</p><p>An oil concession agreement was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> in spring 1933 between the government of King Abdulaziz and the Standard Oil Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. Drilling of the first well began on 30 April 1935, and Dammam No. 1 was abandoned in January 1936 after poor results. Most of the next <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> disappointed. Work on Dammam No. 7 began on 7 December 1936, and by 4 March 1938 it was producing a promising amount of oil. The well, later called the Prosperity Well, showed that the kingdom held very large reserves of hydrocarbons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1950 Dammam had 22,000 inhabitants, and by 2000 759,000. The area was planned from the start on modern principles, with residential and commercial districts separated and broad, straight roads. Dhahran International Airport opened in 1961, and in 1999 commercial flights moved to the larger King Fahd International Airport. King Abdulaziz Port, founded in the late 1940s, is the largest port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and the King Fahd Causeway, 28 kilometres long, links the region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. Saudi Aramco dominates the oil sector, with its headquarters in nearby Dhahran, and industrial cities were built to encourage other industries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dammam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The population reached 32 million in 2022, about 85% of them urban in Riyadh, Jeddah and <b>Dammam</b>, half under 25 and close to 42% immigrants; about 80% of private-sector employees are foreign, many under the kafala system that rights groups link to slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
